       [--rr udpretries] [--vv] [--TT] [--PP] [--VV] [ [--44] | [--66] ] [filename]

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       nnssuuppddaattee  is  used to submit Dynamic DNS Update requests, as defined in
       _R_F_C _2_1_3_6, to a name server. This allows resource records to be added or
       removed from a zone without manually editing the zone  file.  A  single
       update  request can contain requests to add or remove more than one re‐
       source record.

       Zones that are under dynamic control via  nnssuuppddaattee  or  a  DHCP  server
       should  not be edited by hand. Manual edits could conflict with dynamic
       updates and cause data to be lost.

       The resource records that are dynamically added or removed  with  nnssuupp‐‐
       ddaattee  must be in the same zone. Requests are sent to the zone's primary
       server, which is identified by  the  MNAME  field  of  the  zone's  SOA
       record.

       Transaction  signatures can be used to authenticate the Dynamic DNS up‐
       dates. These use the TSIG resource record type described in  _R_F_C  _2_8_4_5,
       the  SIG(0)  record  described in _R_F_C _2_5_3_5 and _R_F_C _2_9_3_1, or GSS-TSIG as
       described in _R_F_C _3_6_4_5.

       TSIG relies on a shared secret that should only be  known  to  nnssuuppddaattee
       and  the  name server. For instance, suitable kkeeyy and sseerrvveerr statements
       are added to ////eettcc//nnaammeedd..ccoonnff so that the name server can associate the
       appropriate secret key and algorithm with the IP address of the  client
       application  that is using TSIG authentication. _d_d_n_s_-_c_o_n_f_g_e_n can gener‐
       ate suitable configuration fragments. nnssuuppddaattee uses the _-_y  or  _-_k  op‐
       tions to provide the TSIG shared secret; these options are mutually ex‐
       clusive.

       SIG(0)  uses  public  key cryptography. To use a SIG(0) key, the public
       key must be stored in a KEY record in a zone served by the name server.

       GSS-TSIG uses Kerberos credentials. Standard GSS-TSIG mode is  switched
       on with the _-_g flag. A non-standards-compliant variant of GSS-TSIG used
       by Windows 2000 can be switched on with the _-_o flag.

